From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 6 Sep 2026 15:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a pairing PIN on screen, and valid, until it is used

raop->pin is set only by the pair-pin-start handler, and consumed by the
first message of pair-setup-pin. Neither half survives a retry, and a
sender retries readily: whenever its user mistypes, takes too long, or --
as iOS does, having paired here before -- offers a remembered password
without asking for a PIN at all. Each attempt is a fresh connection.

So the second attempt finds raop->pin at 0, and

    snprintf(pin, 6, "%04u", raop->pin % 10000);

makes the SRP password "0000". Nothing is displayed, and the PIN the user
is still reading off the screen no longer validates. It is also a way in,
since "0000" is not a secret and is the same on every receiver.

Generate a PIN when there is none, display it on every attempt rather
than only on the one that made it, and hold it until the pairing actually
completes. A PIN the application pinned is held above 9999 and is meant
to outlast a pairing, so that one is left alone.

Signed-off-by: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
---
 lib/raop_handlers.h | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/raop_handlers.h b/lib/raop_handlers.h
--- a/lib/raop_handlers.h
+++ b/lib/raop_handlers.h
@@ -326,10 +326,33 @@
         method = NULL;
         plist_get_string_val(req_user_node, &user);
         logger_log(raop->logger, LOGGER_INFO, "pair-setup-pin:  device_id = %s", user);
+        if (!raop->pin) {
+            /* A sender that has paired here before goes straight to
+             * pair-setup-pin without asking for a PIN first, so there is none
+             * pending. Left alone, raop->pin formats to "0000" below: never
+             * displayed, so it can only be guessed -- and the same on every
+             * receiver. */
+            int pin_4 = random_pin();
+            if (pin_4 < 0) {
+                logger_log(raop->logger, LOGGER_ERR, "Failed to generate random pin");
+            } else {
+                raop->pin = (unsigned short) pin_4 % 10000;
+            }
+        }
         snprintf(pin, 6, "%04u", raop->pin % 10000);
-        if (raop->pin < 10000) {
-            raop->pin = 0;
+
+        /* Show it on every attempt, not only the one that made it. The sender
+         * asks again whenever its user mistypes, takes too long, or -- as iOS
+         * does -- offers a remembered password first, and each attempt is a
+         * fresh connection. The PIN is deliberately not consumed here either:
+         * doing so left the next attempt with none, so what the user was
+         * reading off the screen had already stopped being the answer. */
+        if (raop->callbacks.display_pin) {
+            raop->callbacks.display_pin(raop->callbacks.cls, pin);
         }
+        logger_log(raop->logger, LOGGER_INFO,
+                   "*** CLIENT MUST NOW ENTER PIN = \"%s\" AS AIRPLAY PASSWORD", pin);
+
         int ret = srp_new_user(conn->session, raop->pairing, (const char *) user,
                                (const char *) pin, &salt, &len_salt, &pk, &len_pk);
         plist_mem_free(user);
@@ -418,6 +441,12 @@
             logger_log(raop->logger, LOGGER_DEBUG, "pair-pin-setup success\n");
         }
         pairing_session_set_setup_status(conn->session);
+        /* Paired, so retire the PIN and let the next device asking get a new
+         * one. A PIN the application pinned is held above 9999 and is meant to
+         * outlast a pairing, so leave that one where it is. */
+        if (raop->pin < 10000) {
+            raop->pin = 0;
+        }
         plist_t res_root_node = plist_new_dict();
         plist_t res_epk_node = plist_new_data((const char *) epk, 32);
         plist_t res_authtag_node = plist_new_data((const char *) authtag, 16);
